      },
      {
        "caution_id": "evidence-gap:29862a91b6f487c5c34ae94a",
        "description": "Markov and short-distance assumptions: Model assumptions govern interpretation, but do not certify observed data or authorize an automatic safety block.",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "content_hash": "9acb0f0fcc934fd5974bcb0e7827f5126ac6fd31f11c0be19580441e9f7c5016",
            "end_offset": 7835,
            "evidence_span_id": "evidence-span:5020d0529e55c7860a29286f",
            "exact_text": "Using optimal transport, we calculate couplings between consecutive time points and then infer couplings over longer\ntime-intervals by composing the transport maps between every pair of consecutive intermediate time points. The optimal-transport\ncalculation (i) implicitly assumes that a cell’s fate depends on its current position but not on its previous history\n(i.e., the stochastic process is Markov) and (ii) captures only the time-varying components of the distribution (see Discussion).",
            "locator": {
